---
title: "MCP Events"
description: "Experimental. Deliver events to MCP Events webhook subscriptions, signed with Standard Webhooks."
---

{% callout type="warning" %}
Experimental. This destination type and its behavior may change without notice. It is off by default, and `mcp` destinations are created and refreshed only through the [subscribe endpoints](#subscribe-endpoints-for-an-mcp-server), with the Admin API key. While it is off, stored `mcp` destinations receive no events and lapse at their expiry. Before downgrading to an Outpost version without MCP support, delete every `mcp` destination. Older versions don't know `expires_at`, so expired subscriptions still match events; their deliveries fail and are redelivered, and listing that tenant's destinations returns an error.
{% /callout %}

This implements MCP Events (draft [SEP-3415](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/pull/3415)), an MCP extension for subscribing to events. An `mcp` destination is one MCP Events webhook subscription: a callback URL that receives one topic's events, wrapped in the MCP Events envelope and signed with [Standard Webhooks](https://www.standardwebhooks.com/). Your MCP server creates it on behalf of an agent through the subscribe endpoints, and refreshes or removes it as the agent renews and unsubscribes.

## Enabling MCP Events

{% tabs tabGroup="deployment" %}
{% tab label="Managed" %}

Turn on MCP Events and define the events in [Hookdeck MCP Events settings](https://dashboard.hookdeck.com/settings/project/mcp-events), or set `EXPERIMENTAL_MCP_ENABLED` and `EXPERIMENTAL_MCP_EVENTS_SCHEMA` through the Config API. Your MCP server calls the subscribe endpoints through the Outpost API with the API key.

{% /tab %}
{% tab label="Self-Hosted" %}

Set [`EXPERIMENTAL_MCP_ENABLED`](/docs/outpost/self-hosting/configuration#mcp-events-experimental) to `true`, and define the events with `EXPERIMENTAL_MCP_EVENTS_SCHEMA`.

{% /tab %}
{% /tabs %}

## MCP Destinations

`POST` and `PATCH` on `/tenants/<TENANT_ID>/destinations` refuse `type: "mcp"` for every caller, with `422` and a message that points to the subscribe route. Listing, retrieving, deleting, enabling and disabling `mcp` destinations work as for any destination, and a tenant token can delete its own.

Before a subscription is stored, Outpost sends a verification challenge to the callback URL. If the callback fails it, including with a non-2xx reply, subscribing answers with a `CallbackEndpointError` whose `reason` is `challenge_failed`, `connection_refused`, `timeout` or `tls_error`. A callback that is not `https` (unless allowed below) or that resolves to a non-public address answers `InvalidParams`. If Outpost hits a verification limit, the answer is `ResourceExhausted`. A callback that was already verified for the same tenant, principal and URL is not challenged again for `EXPERIMENTAL_MCP_VERIFICATION_TTL_SECONDS`.

Verification is limited to 10 challenges per tenant and principal per minute, and 120 failed challenges per tenant and callback host per minute.

## Fields

A destination created by the subscribe endpoints has these fields:

| Field | Description |
|-------|-------------|
| `id` | Subscription ID, sent as `X-MCP-Subscription-Id`: derived from the principal, callback URL, event and arguments. |
| `topics` | The subscribed event. |
| `config.url` | Callback URL. `https`, in normalized form (lowercase host, no default port, no credentials); `http` only with `EXPERIMENTAL_MCP_ALLOW_INSECURE_CALLBACKS`, for addresses in `DESTINATIONS_PRIVATE_ADDRESS_ALLOWLIST`, for development. Must resolve to a public address, unless allowed by `DESTINATIONS_PRIVATE_ADDRESS_ALLOWLIST`. |
| `config.principal` | The subject the subscription belongs to, up to 512 bytes. Verification is cached per tenant, principal and URL. |
| `config.arguments` | Canonical JSON object, up to 4 KiB. Must satisfy the event's `inputSchema`. |
| `credentials.secret` | `whsec_` followed by base64 of 24 to 64 bytes. Not shown when the destination is retrieved. |
| `expires_at` | From then on the subscription matches no events and receives no deliveries. |

Subscribing again with a new secret rotates it: the old secret keeps signing for 24 hours alongside the new one.

Each subscription counts toward `MAX_DESTINATIONS_PER_TENANT`. When a tenant is at the limit, subscribing first removes the tenant's expired ones.

## Event Definitions

Event definitions list the events an MCP server can offer and the arguments an agent can filter each one by. They use the format of an MCP `events/list` result:

```json
{
  "events": [
    {
      "name": "order.created",
      "description": "A new order was placed.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "status": { "type": "string", "enum": ["paid", "pending"] }
        }
      },
      "payloadSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "order_id": { "type": "string" },
          "status": { "type": "string" },
          "total": { "type": "number" }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "order.shipped",
      "description": "An order left the warehouse.",
      "payloadSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "order_id": { "type": "string" },
          "carrier": { "type": "string" }
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

An agent subscribing to `order.created` can pass `{"status": "paid"}` and receives only the orders whose `status` is `paid`. `order.shipped` takes no arguments.

- `name` is a topic and is unique in the list. With `TOPICS` set, it must be one of them. Wildcards aren't allowed.
- `payloadSchema` is a required JSON Schema object that describes the event's `data`.
- `inputSchema` is an optional JSON Schema object that lists the arguments under `properties`. Without it the event takes no arguments, and `GET /mcp/events/list` returns `{"type": "object", "properties": {}}`.
- Each argument is a string, number, integer or boolean, and names a top-level field of the payload with a compatible type. Outpost delivers an event only if every argument equals the field of the same name. `additionalProperties` must be `false` or absent, and an argument has no `default`.
- `description`, `delivery` and `_meta` are optional. When `delivery` is set it must include `webhook`.

Invalid definitions are reported with the event and the field.

{% tabs tabGroup="deployment" %}
{% tab label="Managed" %}

Import the JSON (or YAML) in [Hookdeck MCP Events settings](https://dashboard.hookdeck.com/settings/project/mcp-events), or edit the events there. The same format is what Export produces.

{% /tab %}
{% tab label="Self-Hosted" %}

Set the JSON in [`EXPERIMENTAL_MCP_EVENTS_SCHEMA`](/docs/outpost/self-hosting/configuration#mcp-events-experimental), inline or as the path of a JSON or YAML file. In the YAML config, `experimental.mcp.events_schema` takes the mapping inline or a file path.

{% /tab %}
{% /tabs %}

A subscription must target a defined event: `NotFound` otherwise, so with no definitions every subscription is refused. `config.arguments` must satisfy the event's `inputSchema`, and an argument that isn't listed under its `properties` is refused. `filter` can't be set on these subscriptions. Checks run when a subscription is created.

`GET /mcp/events/list` (Admin API key) returns the configured list in order as an MCP `events/list` result (`resultType: "complete"`), with `delivery` set to `["webhook"]` on each event and `nextCursor` when there are more. `topics=a,b` restricts the list to those events, and `limit` and `cursor` page through it. Without MCP enabled or any events configured, the route returns `404`. If the definitions are invalid, Outpost still starts and logs the error; this route and the subscribe endpoints answer `503` and the reason is logged, and existing `mcp` destinations keep delivering.

## Subscribe Endpoints for an MCP Server

An MCP server that exposes `events/subscribe` and `events/unsubscribe` forwards them to Outpost, which creates and removes the `mcp` destinations. With MCP enabled, the Admin API key can call `POST /tenants/<TENANT_ID>/mcp/events/subscribe` and `POST /tenants/<TENANT_ID>/mcp/events/unsubscribe` with `{"principal": ..., "params": ...}`, where `params` are the MCP request's params as received. The answer is the MCP result, or a `422` with an `mcp_error` (`code`, `message`, and `data` where the MCP Events spec defines it) to return as the JSON-RPC error.

The MCP server authenticates its users and sends each user's `principal`. Outpost does not authenticate the principal: it trusts the caller holding the Admin API key, takes the tenant from the path, and creates it if missing. Subscriptions are scoped by tenant and principal: only the principal that created a subscription can refresh or remove it, and callback verification is cached per tenant and principal. Before forwarding a subscribe, the MCP server checks that the principal may receive that event with those arguments, as the spec requires; Outpost enforces only `allowed_topics`. The MCP server also handles the MCP transport: advertising the events extension and checking the `Mcp-Name` header.

- A new subscription is checked against the event definitions and verified as described in [MCP Destinations](#mcp-destinations). Subscribing creates the tenant if it does not exist, even when the subscribe then fails.
- `allowed_topics` on the subscribe call restricts the principal's events: omitted allows every event, an empty list allows none. A subscription to a refused event answers `Forbidden`.
- Subscribing again with the same principal, event, arguments and callback URL refreshes the subscription: same `id`, new expiry, the new secret, re-enabled if it was suspended. Once a subscription has expired, subscribing starts a new one.
- The lifetime follows the agent's `ttlMs`, within `EXPERIMENTAL_MCP_SUBSCRIPTION_TTL_MIN_SECONDS` and `EXPERIMENTAL_MCP_SUBSCRIPTION_TTL_MAX_SECONDS`. No `ttlMs` gets `EXPERIMENTAL_MCP_SUBSCRIPTION_TTL_DEFAULT_SECONDS`; `null` gets the maximum.

## Delivery

Each event is sent as an HTTP POST:

```
POST /mcp-events/abc123 HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json
webhook-id: msg_x1yQ3o8mT5vZk2cJ0aNwXg
webhook-timestamp: 1790000000
webhook-signature: v1,K5oZfzN95Z9UVu1EsfQmfVNQhnkZ2/d+...
X-MCP-Subscription-Id: sub_12345

{"eventId":"evt_123","name":"order.created","timestamp":"2026-10-27T12:00:00Z","data":{"order_id":"ord_42"},"cursor":null}
```

- `cursor` is always `null`: there is no replay, and Outpost doesn't send `gap` or `terminated` envelopes.
- `webhook-id` is random, set when the event is matched to the subscription, and the same on every automatic retry. A manual retry sends a new one. Receivers should dedupe retries on `webhook-id`. A dedupe on `eventId` must be scoped to the subscription (`X-MCP-Subscription-Id`): one event is delivered once per matching subscription.
- `webhook-timestamp` and `webhook-signature` are computed again on every attempt. During a secret rotation, the signature header carries one signature per valid secret.
- Redirects are never followed.
- Deliveries and verification challenges connect directly, checking each address at connection time, or through the `DESTINATIONS_PROXY_URL` forward proxy when it is set. With a proxy, connections go to the proxy and aren't checked: the proxy resolves the callback host and must refuse private addresses itself. The check when a subscription is created or updated still runs. `HTTP_PROXY`/`HTTPS_PROXY` don't apply.

## Retries

A `2xx` response is a success. Any other response, a network error or a refused address is a failed attempt and is retried on `EXPERIMENTAL_MCP_RETRY_SCHEDULE` (`RETRY_SCHEDULE` and `MAX_RETRY_LIMIT` don't apply to `mcp`). A `3xx` is a failure with its status as the attempt code.

These are not retried, and don't count toward auto-disabling the destination: a `410` or `413` response, and an event whose envelope exceeds 256 KiB (attempt code `payload_too_large`, no request sent).
